{:answer/id #uuid "5d4aab7f-7df3-55bc-a651-aedd2ef3e896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/70/70b68fb23c61f678b381699790f4d8be8d90d17cfe5d00d760ce6cc069c4f4b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394409} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5f7c3a-b1c7-55dd-85d8-b17e8a91a5ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6d/6d42feee54531cf1381876a908128628cbd2a65ce3701d501bc61ebf63e25c27.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394410} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf5114e-5807-5740-8d2f-20815b96b415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c0/c0dfba56ed4c0ceb0b1739304c562040a2a69044117665bba5bf4fc2f506044f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394411} {:answer/id #uuid "b0968c92-858c-58df-afd7-5150f3e16b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f6/f66eb5d6fe0c87b68433ccc1ac79738881a4a73e2d57e21c3adfe1478c3af329.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394412} {:answer/id #uuid "8848cfd9-bc16-57a2-b268-a323dd18d84b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d6d3778b4ef2e343c0aae56478a040eec0d0fb51fda7e997fade27a74ffcb146.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb5f7c3a-b1c7-55dd-85d8-b17e8a91a5ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6d/6d42feee54531cf1381876a908128628cbd2a65ce3701d501bc61ebf63e25c27.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394410} :answer-field/id #uuid "bef28f6f-5888-57ec-9c2e-9b032950742a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de57fef1-dcc7-5e38-9a5e-c9e487f617fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-82529" :question/problem "![](images/42/4229e1e8c92803a4d51043442a8bc65a6560e11a1e7559af7ec4246fe3005e75.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation that acts on vectors in ${R}^{2}$ as follows (in the order given):\n\n- it shears vectors by a shear factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $y$-direction, and then\n\n- reflects the result in the $x$-axis.\n\nWhat is the image of the unit square shown above under the action of the linear transformation?" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the shear by a shear factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $y$-direction:\n\n![](images/d6/d6d3778b4ef2e343c0aae56478a040eec0d0fb51fda7e997fade27a74ffcb146.png)\n\nThen, we apply the reflection in the $x$-axis:\n\n![](images/6d/6d42feee54531cf1381876a908128628cbd2a65ce3701d501bc61ebf63e25c27.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "833ac63c-235b-5059-ab7c-56eb1e44d79e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Second Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bdc6138-01e7-5dca-90c2-ee5ef772f650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348685} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5f7895-83a7-5868-921c-57c50450ef1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348975} {:answer/id #uuid "69962bbe-75b8-5ae4-99aa-4f7910f71965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349425} {:answer/id #uuid "7df6bfdf-a463-5e56-a7b0-8cc61b55f17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.07" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349601} {:answer/id #uuid "b60fe42a-6aaa-59ac-b35e-8ac4d19e26ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bdc6138-01e7-5dca-90c2-ee5ef772f650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348685} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c0ed589-e9f7-57d6-8078-aa521972d708" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61e93f3c-1103-58d6-a5e4-4e5649a9f688" :question/math-academy-id "q-8258" :question/problem "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-2+5\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/fc/fc6a2568ba77ce043ae0d48840ed4707b3e629b8a52c4b5f182c1e23a249fa44.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<\\pi .$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan⁡(\\frac{5}{2}) \\\\  & ≈1.190\\text{ }29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)≈\\pi -1.190\\text{ }29≈1.95\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083207 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68b775fe-ef39-53c3-b45e-fea1b72ccc59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349409} {:answer/id #uuid "7865e039-341a-5613-a2f9-06ba73a4045e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2+{sin}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349558} {:answer/id #uuid "920839f3-40ee-5e07-abc8-6c2ae62058fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4+{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349753} {:answer/id #uuid "9d65eabc-ea03-5514-81ec-28cf5661ff3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349853} {:answer/id #uuid "f242e833-1bea-549c-978c-d0a552fd87ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68b775fe-ef39-53c3-b45e-fea1b72ccc59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349409} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a38a808-d225-556c-a4d2-394d7d04d085" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "63c4bab2-2e6d-520c-adaf-aa9c6200efdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-82612" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}={cos}^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=3-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083207 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f899358-d5f2-5466-9131-304bdebe0e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{2}-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348721} {:answer/id #uuid "5533d49f-4da5-5199-8b25-bdd14de23299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1-{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349226} {:answer/id #uuid "7afc79cd-0676-5d65-9f25-1357a3d00d57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349577} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea80a1f-ac19-503a-8de4-6824ef0e5522" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2+\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349862} {:answer/id #uuid "f784e3f7-6f7c-52d3-b9bf-ae54f06d2517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f899358-d5f2-5466-9131-304bdebe0e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{2}-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348721} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9da7025-13b5-52e9-a34f-6a7e72d91f8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18227bf5-d04e-5451-bd52-00e552502d65" :question/math-academy-id "q-82616" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\sqrt{2}-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087718 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a6128-22df-5a02-96fd-7c8cabf8f5f6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Complex Number in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ef259f9-6220-5719-b73f-f9e14cba53a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=13(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.176))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348863} {:answer/id #uuid "305d1949-c98f-5233-bbfc-3a89cbfc0c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=15(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.723)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.723))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348876} {:answer/id #uuid "6a6d4a2e-e6b9-5883-a2d2-4f5ef0dac7a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{15}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.486)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.486))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349428} {:answer/id #uuid "8e3107c4-9eb8-5fe0-9fae-1d7e36aa0717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{13}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.176))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349728} {:answer/id #uuid "b1280a6d-c454-5f65-93e2-6c3b8cbf0d5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=13(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.641)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.641))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ef259f9-6220-5719-b73f-f9e14cba53a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=13(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.176))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348863} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e03d1a2-30fd-5e5d-a7b1-322a0564c17b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d310c8c-3e11-5226-89ae-78e76ac29fd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-8263" :question/problem "Express $z=5+12\\text{i}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give $\\theta $ in radians to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "In polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=5+12\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=5$ is positive and $y=12$ is positive, $z$ lies in the first quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{12}{5}) \\\\  & ≈1.176,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z=5+12\\text{i}$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=13(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.176))\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083207 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44ebe5ba-c96f-5b0e-ac7c-73a2ac572222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3\\sqrt{x}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349072} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7ed6a1-14d9-57f2-b7ac-47a688b9ae1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349124} {:answer/id #uuid "960dc7a9-4ae3-5152-b870-7fd599b98f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349794} {:answer/id #uuid "b78a10c9-f058-562f-a821-e1178f6ed9fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350071} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e9f9fb-81dd-58cd-aaca-17003a942de9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a7ed6a1-14d9-57f2-b7ac-47a688b9ae1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349124} :answer-field/id #uuid "01d9d2ba-3067-5544-bbd3-b967b20c10ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68d17fec-293a-5a95-8794-4f5a6e83dd1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-82639" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+9x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+9x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=9x\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm 3\\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm 3\\int \\sqrt{x}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=-2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33159d0b-24b5-587e-89e1-b545c2be59c4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial of a Given Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e2619de-e2e4-5d96-81b3-945eaa80775b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348568} {:answer/id #uuid "28e263a4-e9c1-5152-bc0b-70d80ade00cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{3/2}(1+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348798} {:answer/id #uuid "37223aea-88bf-5c4a-b3d4-99196518ae2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{1/2}(1-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348944} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3ec5f2-e3be-5be2-9c82-439f235b5142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{9}{4}x-\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348971} {:answer/id #uuid "a7737e7e-c654-5e57-8f8a-b76e619d290b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{3/2}(1-\\frac{9}{4}x+\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7737e7e-c654-5e57-8f8a-b76e619d290b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{3/2}(1-\\frac{9}{4}x+\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349938} :answer-field/id #uuid "7126cc2f-aeb2-5b19-a11e-65c74ef22d11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0dcf3f88-1f5e-5b29-b98b-e45db61b75ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-8269" :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=(2-3x)^{3/2}$ about $x=0$ is" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(2-3x)^{3/2} & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =(2-0)^{3/2}={2}^{3/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{9}{2}(2-3x)^{1/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{9}{2}(2-0)^{1/2}=-9\\cdot {2}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{27}{4}(2-3x)^{-1/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =27\\cdot {2}^{-5/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & ={2}^{3/2}+(-9\\cdot {2}^{-1/2})x+(27\\cdot {2}^{-5/2})(\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{3/2}(1-\\frac{9}{4}x+\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083343 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83d52a4-6f69-5607-8753-e96c13bd7209" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting One of the Limits of an Improper Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "136126e5-8f97-5ea6-ad15-611f0e714006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({\\int }_{-2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348614} {:answer/id #uuid "a3dc4d41-f8c6-5c5c-92fe-94d9872501a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to (-2)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349911} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f1d55f-41be-53f0-a423-d5176e710370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to (-8)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350179} {:answer/id #uuid "c7948ef6-c863-5cf3-874d-cb1661836aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to {3}^{-}}({\\int }_{-2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350204} {:answer/id #uuid "e81a3fa7-f30d-582a-b310-3b27b5ceb6d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3dc4d41-f8c6-5c5c-92fe-94d9872501a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to (-2)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349911} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3a408db-bd3a-5c4c-bd47-d249415b9a2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37133a14-12b0-54f8-a249-3f8317024d5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-82701" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}+8}?$" :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-2,3):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,3]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to (-2)^{+}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to (-2)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083343 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83d52a4-6f69-5607-8753-e96c13bd7209" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting One of the Limits of an Improper Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "257c192a-1184-5538-8799-052600599292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to (-3)^{-}}({\\int }_{-6}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348768} {:answer/id #uuid "4248b2d0-c7d8-53a9-88eb-f36956eb0075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to -\\infty }({\\int }_{b}^{-3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349048} {:answer/id #uuid "c19db006-dd4b-5c37-a3cd-9b1356f216a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }({\\int }_{-6}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350163} {:answer/id #uuid "eacd9fca-980f-555e-83e1-5fa0fe01a832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to (-6)^{+}}({\\int }_{b}^{-3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350498} {:answer/id #uuid "ff96f359-7424-5fef-aa47-2ba5c9a2ba00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to {3}^{-}}({\\int }_{-6}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "257c192a-1184-5538-8799-052600599292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to (-3)^{-}}({\\int }_{-6}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348768} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffc4024c-016a-557e-a841-834437673b41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d29116a1-5886-5193-a3da-549480287a4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-82704" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{-6}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}?$" :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-6,-3):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-3)^{-}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [-6,b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to (-3)^{-}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{-3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to (-3)^{-}}({\\int }_{-6}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083202 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf1d89ed-0d2a-5759-9e6e-354957f7dabc" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Improper Integral of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ceb2c6b-df6c-5404-9959-1c846a9ba373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348558} {:answer/id #uuid "24e27e52-e224-5d9b-b321-6ea5c47a93fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348764} {:answer/id #uuid "2c753f60-894f-535c-927a-b58209e9958f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348836} {:answer/id #uuid "8755d9db-0e80-512f-9a65-07511622a999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349680} {:answer/id #uuid "d24b2e6f-0173-54a9-a57c-423cba29ae97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8755d9db-0e80-512f-9a65-07511622a999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349680} :answer-field/id #uuid "4935c80a-5b19-57d0-a02f-c974a31cf18f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "24585a55-ea54-501a-939f-0ada5b5a8d4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-82842" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral  ${\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=x-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{u}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u||}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2}{a}| \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11647cdd-6315-5718-9425-88f4fb93faa7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor of Degree Higher Than Two"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6121498e-8557-54d4-b289-9f80ba8961dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x+1}+\\frac{3}{(x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349336} {:answer/id #uuid "8b9281ae-c8e9-56da-ab9c-863720a1114d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{x+1}+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349710} {:answer/id #uuid "a44ca2e0-c6ce-53cc-aaeb-4b2cb6dafdc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|-\\frac{6}{(x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349914} {:answer/id #uuid "bb624e7c-d64d-5b7e-ad3a-3cf7959c6e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+\\frac{2}{x+1}+\\frac{3}{(x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350113} {:answer/id #uuid "efaea845-bc7b-5b72-afb0-67459f2ff8c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|-\\frac{3}{x+1}+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b9281ae-c8e9-56da-ab9c-863720a1114d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{x+1}+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349710} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1d56f1e-a189-57a3-b37e-5b074591b80b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1af827e0-397a-561e-854f-4bfe4e074477" :question/math-academy-id "q-82892" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3x+1}{(x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+1}{(x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x+1}+\\frac{B}{(x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x+1)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+1)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n3x+1=A(x+1)^{2}+B(x+1)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-1)+1 & =A(-1+1)^{2}+B(-1+1)+C \\\\ -2 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)+1 & =A(0+1)^{2}+B(0+1)+C \\\\ 1 & =A+B-2 \\\\ A+B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-2$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-2)+1 & =A(-2+1)^{2}+B(-2+1)+C \\\\ -5 & =A-B-2 \\\\ A-B & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=-3$ and $A+B=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =-3+3 \\\\ 2A & =0 \\\\ A & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+B & =3 \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+1}{(x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{3}{(x+1)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+1}{(x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{(x+1)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{x+1}+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11647cdd-6315-5718-9425-88f4fb93faa7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor of Degree Higher Than Two"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c16586d-cc25-53cc-9362-6437b79c060a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|-\\frac{4}{x+2}+\\frac{2}{(x+2)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349714} {:answer/id #uuid "998be8ec-a9f8-5319-9eef-2aae1574b0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+2}+\\frac{2}{(x+2)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349821} {:answer/id #uuid "adea4ada-7cc7-5da3-98b6-914d4acd1442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\frac{2}{(x+2)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349984} {:answer/id #uuid "bef94dca-73fa-5d10-89d2-be9a6b332a16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|-\\frac{1}{x+2}+\\frac{2}{(x+2)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350141} {:answer/id #uuid "f5a58b23-169d-5e0d-b12a-3d71909e57c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|-\\frac{4}{x+2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adea4ada-7cc7-5da3-98b6-914d4acd1442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\frac{2}{(x+2)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349984} :answer-field/id #uuid "21898db2-5323-5985-8c96-07b477ebe19f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e554a089-5768-5395-81cb-0926b256840b" :question/math-academy-id "q-82894" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{(x+2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x+2}+\\frac{B}{(x+2)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x+2)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+2)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4x=A(x+2)^{2}+B(x+2)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{2}+4(-2) & =A(-2+2)^{2}+B(-2+2)+C \\\\ -4 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}+4(0) & =A(0+2)^{2}+B(0+2)+C \\\\ 0 & =4A+2B-4 \\\\ 2A+B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{2}+4(-1) & =A(-1+2)^{2}+B(-1+2)+C \\\\ -3 & =A+B-4 \\\\ A+B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the equations $2A+B=2$ and $A+B=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2-1 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+B & =1 \\\\ B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{(x+2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{4}{(x+2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\frac{2}{(x+2)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fa50117-feef-5fb7-b4c7-8d5074635199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348870} {:answer/id #uuid "452eaa99-839b-51b9-a716-4597a4aef31a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349076} {:answer/id #uuid "74fe7c6f-36b4-5a32-8184-47101e4f7568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349521} {:answer/id #uuid "80940eb3-3340-5a50-8f91-c8c22404f7b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349628} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b526ac-6987-52e6-b725-e6fce574b42d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80940eb3-3340-5a50-8f91-c8c22404f7b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349628} :answer-field/id #uuid "aae6146f-444d-5de3-893c-8276bf7fa71f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61f94262-435d-5c53-971b-8da63f6d82a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-8309" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}$ that satisfies $y(0)=0.$ Assume that $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}<y<\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)} \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{C}_{1}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}=2C.$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find ${C}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+{C}_{1} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y) & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the above equation as\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096699 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4ab07a7-d1ed-5f44-8915-09e7b2418825" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution of a Differential Equation With a Given Condition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dc989a8-2c75-5f1c-9659-077b9b5ceeed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349599} {:answer/id #uuid "90451e36-0f3c-542c-9624-ec09beef5517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349742} {:answer/id #uuid "96633e09-e5d2-5a83-a534-6acb8fbe9b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349795} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d311c7-63bd-56a8-90ae-daf55b933be8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350028} {:answer/id #uuid "eec3251e-786c-5a1a-9eba-fcc26b7bd286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96633e09-e5d2-5a83-a534-6acb8fbe9b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349795} :answer-field/id #uuid "83e5ea7c-8d82-5535-96fc-e39fd184f9e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d35cfdf-da63-5e6d-990c-a2e5c83f1725" :question/math-academy-id "q-8311" :question/problem "Find the specific solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4=0$ given that $y(-1)=4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-4x+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $y=-4x+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $y(-1)=4$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =-4\\cdot (-1)+C \\\\ 4 & =4+C \\\\ 0 & =C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-4x\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a78860ea-192f-5df9-aca9-4f67ec6ffefe" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Given Part Of the Partial Fraction Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3315d6e2-f3af-584e-be74-84ae148139b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348903} {:answer/id #uuid "3c660eb8-61f8-5a34-b9b3-6b5c1677af80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348994} {:answer/id #uuid "a0efe850-d002-5fc4-b645-9f9c8a2c379e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349874} {:answer/id #uuid "a494eb50-5b65-5342-9124-653bae4b7fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349916} {:answer/id #uuid "cbecc3dc-85b5-5efe-8bfe-9f8f5ea1443b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4|+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a494eb50-5b65-5342-9124-653bae4b7fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349916} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c34c280-b856-57e7-aff8-0f9a68ba3d76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3d16f22-84af-5aa2-bf4e-f306446b007f" :question/math-academy-id "q-83161" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)}=\\frac{Ax+1}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{1}{x-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the constant $A.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x+3 & =(Ax+1)(x-1)+{x}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2}+2+3 & =(A(2)+1)(2-1)+{2}^{2}+4 \\\\ 9 & =2A+1+8 \\\\ 9 & =2A+9 \\\\ A & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{0x+1}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{1}{x-1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088417 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "865035a9-dfe6-51bf-a5dc-624240267541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087156} {:answer/id #uuid "981d3229-34da-5547-9622-76e7a57b9b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087157} {:answer/id #uuid "ff08f834-c93c-58b6-a6eb-be4ab302b1ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087158} {:answer/id #uuid "5826ab39-99cb-5f0d-a91a-c8e47d4e7431" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087159} {:answer/id #uuid "58fc334c-401e-58ca-8084-7bbee1c81411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087160}] :answer-field/id #uuid "212166f2-0485-5ec6-aaa6-538998150651" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087155} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9010786-eb95-569b-8bf6-50b7c4d11251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396198} {:answer/id #uuid "9017ed40-b00d-55a3-877f-c3d4b075da7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396199} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a1da3a-abde-5c78-94f6-9beb1ca8472a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396200} {:answer/id #uuid "8a2fc306-e79b-5a44-8145-c511e597d027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396201} {:answer/id #uuid "8b5eb027-48d1-5f9c-9af9-618b997023bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9010786-eb95-569b-8bf6-50b7c4d11251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396198} :answer-field/id #uuid "169533db-26aa-5a83-a7a7-fa42593fd137" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9a96319-3bb3-5dd4-a3ac-27f5effab07d" :question/math-academy-id "q-8331" :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\nII. $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\nIII. $T$ is a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}ky \\\\ kx\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ x\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068748 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "116c34e0-5cfd-5bb3-964d-9f2713592207" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function With an Irreducible Trinomial Quadratic Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "146d90b3-5c9d-5707-a406-0a9382d46855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}-4x-3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348621} {:answer/id #uuid "281a60ee-7789-534b-95a2-91d514a22a70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+\\frac{1}{2({x}^{2}-4x-3)}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348793} {:answer/id #uuid "32e9fad0-e594-54e4-aa9b-28a2b1a366f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+3}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}-4x-3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348901} {:answer/id #uuid "d38e1ec9-6e77-5bcf-910d-a08c7d3657c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}-4x-3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350305} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5dfce4-5487-5c82-997c-f390363c48e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+3}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}-4x-3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d38e1ec9-6e77-5bcf-910d-a08c7d3657c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}-4x-3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350305} :answer-field/id #uuid "62a82bb8-91d2-5271-9a80-81d19c7f9b98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7e6be0c4-0435-506d-8ce1-14ee1c45fe6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-83367" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-9}{(x+3)({x}^{2}-4x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a discriminant that is not a perfect square:\n\n$$\nD=(-4)^{2}-4(1)(-3)=28\n$$\n\nWe have that $\\sqrt{28}$ is not a rational number, which implies that the quadratic factor doesn't factor over the rational numbers.\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-9}{(x+3)({x}^{2}-4x-3)}=\\frac{A}{x+3}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}-4x-3}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+3)({x}^{2}-4x-3),$ and we get\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-3x-9=A({x}^{2}-4x-3)+(Bx+C)(x+3)\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=1,B=1$ and $C=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-9}{(x+3)({x}^{2}-4x-3)}=\\frac{1}{x+3}+\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4x-3}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-9}{(x+3)({x}^{2}-4x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x+3}+\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4x-3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4x-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}-4x-3.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2(x-2)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}-4x-3|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-9}{(x+3)({x}^{2}-4x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}-4x-3|+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de71fcb6-36ca-581a-92ea-47c1d0c6bf10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Product of Functions Using Standard Maclaurin Expansions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6684cb3-66f7-5670-a618-240321438de8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-{x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{120}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404247} {:answer/id #uuid "d8eb2141-deaf-5745-8f39-c0df69c4795f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{12}+\\frac{{x}^{7}}{60}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404248} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f058db-10d3-5127-a407-810a87d6f495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=1-x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}-⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404249} {:answer/id #uuid "6537e3c8-b8ab-55e5-9855-26f836f4373a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{24}+\\frac{{x}^{9}}{96}-⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404250} {:answer/id #uuid "1ccc3d10-6b2b-5fed-b615-462c22afdb65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{7}}{120}-⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ccc3d10-6b2b-5fed-b615-462c22afdb65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{7}}{120}-⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404251} :answer-field/id #uuid "38f38032-483e-5ebe-a2ba-4fc05febd614" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83cee5be-d033-528b-aa1a-c2f13225ee96" :question/math-academy-id "q-83429" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5!}-⋯ \\\\  & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{120}-⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get an expansion for ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we multiply this series by ${x}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & ={x}^{2}(x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{120}-⋯) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{7}}{120}-⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9a12b49-8f77-5480-a3c7-c291e9549af1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity Vector at a Point in Time Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f349ccaf-635c-5643-92d8-6ff7fffcacfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨121,15⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394492} {:answer/id #uuid "24cc6a45-71de-50bc-a394-3e20bc5a7ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨32,-49⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394493} {:answer/id #uuid "3ebb1330-51e4-5cb7-af9d-c81f72807962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨85,-8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394494} {:answer/id #uuid "b23e6a9c-17ef-592d-98ac-3399ef06798e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨-66,21⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394495} {:answer/id #uuid "1e8efdf2-2841-5402-b342-6b954fa1a59f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨112,10⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f349ccaf-635c-5643-92d8-6ff7fffcacfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨121,15⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394492} :answer-field/id #uuid "51f50a45-adc6-5b16-876f-809ec791c11f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb1ed2a5-10d4-520a-8be1-03271b56ffd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-83497" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨12(1+2t)^{3},9(1-3t)^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨1,6⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨12(1+2t)^{3},9(1-3t)^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 12(1+2t)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 9(1-3t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}(1+2t)^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-(1-3t)^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨1,6⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}(1+2(0))^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-(1-3(0))^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,6⟩ & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}+{C}_{1},\\,-1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${C}_{2}=7.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}(1+2t)^{4}-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-(1-3t)^{3}+7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}(1+2(1))^{4}-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-(1-3(1))^{3}+7⟩ \\\\  & =⟨121,15⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351515 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02a9771f-af0a-5af7-94b7-9d6cb6f060d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points at Which a Particle is Stationary Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01dba6f4-ef69-5263-83c9-d6b5cf4e7e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348470} {:answer/id #uuid "2e0c721b-99e3-5815-bb90-817ac3d18f80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No stationary points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348851} {:answer/id #uuid "37049c4e-0537-5e70-81a4-6f6c66989874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348943} {:answer/id #uuid "7b859de9-1268-5c7e-b4ab-f28252eddf3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349580} {:answer/id #uuid "a2764333-d334-5787-88d5-b83eedaccbe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2764333-d334-5787-88d5-b83eedaccbe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349895} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba7fab13-0862-5060-9578-091695a5b4a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8317a43-8d81-53a8-b37c-7e3e16624a80" :question/math-academy-id "q-83506" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩,\\,0\\le t<\\pi .\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨1,1⟩$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=⟨1,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=1$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=0, \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=0,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129202 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f0085-01ab-5d16-884e-49200bc7ac14" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Given the CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e43f0bf-6626-5307-a064-a72be2e80a70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348856} {:answer/id #uuid "98cd6778-156a-541d-8e22-df64bec870e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349815} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf92353-21bf-51c5-8337-6fef75f61aaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349836} {:answer/id #uuid "c21447d6-9c20-50ba-9608-5c974f5d9781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350169} {:answer/id #uuid "d0818283-dcbb-59bd-84f5-15236241b86c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c21447d6-9c20-50ba-9608-5c974f5d9781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350169} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ac4354d-99af-5654-a4d2-0d3920502f14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "33a751a7-7f9e-5b67-a685-e5ad79051983" :question/math-academy-id "q-83623" :question/problem "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(y)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(y)$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $0.8$ | $1.0$ |" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $Y$ is a function $F(y)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(y)=P(Y\\le y).\n$$\n\nTo compute the probability of a random variable $Y$ over an interval, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(a<Y\\le b)=F(b)-F(a).\n$$\n\nHere, we want to compute $P(\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}).$ However, this interval is stated in the form $a\\le Y\\le b,$ not $a<Y\\le b.$\n\nSo first, we must convert the interval $\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}$ to the form $a<Y\\le b.$ Looking at the given possible values of $Y,$ we see that $\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y$ is equivalent to $0<Y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}) & =P(0<Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =F(\\frac{3}{2})-F(0) \\\\  & =0.8-0.1 \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c3fc090-22e5-5f05-9634-b8c645cf5700" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Expected Value Given the Frequency of a Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c5ac2b8-8998-5ea4-8f48-222394847936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348993} {:answer/id #uuid "3de78b59-5d7d-5dbc-83c1-87b37fef9111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349008} {:answer/id #uuid "48984eb0-6d19-5571-bff6-6f66c9509c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349107} {:answer/id #uuid "6f32402b-10b2-5fb4-ad86-9759afeb675f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349465} {:answer/id #uuid "b446e63a-9150-529f-a576-373854567da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b446e63a-9150-529f-a576-373854567da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350040} :answer-field/id #uuid "000354d3-3c2f-5dd1-82a3-33944501c003" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fbc0a31-7ccf-5545-90f4-14b6b96a81d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-83641" :question/problem "Several egg cartons were checked for cracked eggs. The number of cracked eggs in each carton was recorded below. If $N$ is the number of cracked eggs in a carton, what is the expected value of $N?$\n\n| Number of Cartons | $10$ | $7$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Cracked Eggs | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the cracked eggs, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of cartons is $10+7+2+1=20,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(N=0)=f(0) & =\\frac{10}{20}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ P(N=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{7}{20} \\\\ P(N=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{2}{20}=\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ P(N=3)=f(3) & =\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{20}$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{20}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[N] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{1}{2})+1(\\frac{7}{20})+2(\\frac{1}{10})+3(\\frac{1}{20}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{7}{20}+\\frac{2}{10}+\\frac{3}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{20}+\\frac{4}{20}+\\frac{3}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{7+4+3}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10} \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c3fc090-22e5-5f05-9634-b8c645cf5700" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Expected Value Given the Frequency of a Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "169125f0-3e4b-5c6a-b5a1-89b777b83f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348635} {:answer/id #uuid "37ec9671-ab78-519f-b1e7-71d5da25779b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348951} {:answer/id #uuid "55449f33-7665-51fe-9702-f5d7e4e864fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349228} {:answer/id #uuid "73dd5657-1c1c-53ad-ad9d-ac117ac7883a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349511} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2f7a50-e7e8-5a25-86d8-2f5cc19d264e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "169125f0-3e4b-5c6a-b5a1-89b777b83f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348635} :answer-field/id #uuid "c89f0117-9472-553a-ba2d-6662ba158e3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "697d7677-790c-5ef6-9fc8-07067ff8df87" :question/math-academy-id "q-83642" :question/problem "Several children were asked their age. The age of each child (in years) was recorded below. If $X$ is the age of a randomly selected child, what is the expected value of $X?$\n\n| Age | $10$ | $11$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Number of Children | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the children's ages, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of children is $2+4+4=10,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=10)=f(10) & =\\frac{2}{10}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ P(X=11)=f(11) & =\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ P(X=12)=f(12) & =\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $10$ | $11$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =10\\cdot f(10)+11\\cdot f(11)+12\\cdot f(12) \\\\  & =10(\\frac{1}{5})+11(\\frac{2}{5})+12(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =2+\\frac{22}{5}+\\frac{24}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5}+\\frac{22}{5}+\\frac{24}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{10+22+24}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{5} \\\\  & =11.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e448e28-466c-5051-9ada-5e030e6a8ff7" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Standard Deviation of a Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1909d2b-25e6-5830-9a29-d5c2b4d19bf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371148} {:answer/id #uuid "200c82ee-8535-578a-b8ea-4eddd2b8125b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371149} {:answer/id #uuid "0c6bd20c-78d9-5fd4-ac77-fead83f6ab5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371150} {:answer/id #uuid "be41e9bc-28fa-51a7-a288-71452cdec58b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371151} {:answer/id #uuid "1782f285-6adb-5361-8525-39975729d1c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be41e9bc-28fa-51a7-a288-71452cdec58b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371151} :answer-field/id #uuid "486d8dd1-c27c-5f9e-9d9f-3777fbed8181" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e518a99-0566-5086-b5fd-28824f354071" :question/math-academy-id "q-83712" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a roll of an unfair four-sided die that follows the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below. What is the standard deviation of $X?$\n\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |" :question/worked-solution "The standard deviation of a random variable $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]},\n$$\n\nwhere the variance can be computed using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{6})+2(\\frac{1}{3})+3(\\frac{1}{6})+4(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={1}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{2}^{2}\\cdot f(2)+{3}^{2}\\cdot f(3)+{4}^{2}\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{6})+4(\\frac{1}{3})+9(\\frac{1}{6})+16(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{3}-{(\\frac{8}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{3}-\\frac{64}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100937 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "039f9f0b-70dd-5360-9240-2260ff706c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Coefficient in a Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94996d93-0d2b-5ad2-9e71-7de2292aee5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349776} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b85c93-400a-5fe3-adf5-f59817b3a2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350165} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b16b34-3d73-5954-bff0-00e45b66cec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350347} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e1c382-deee-55db-8614-2ee9d7a5f7e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350453} {:answer/id #uuid "f27d74ba-36a6-5d39-9284-008012a89045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94996d93-0d2b-5ad2-9e71-7de2292aee5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349776} :answer-field/id #uuid "15377768-284e-56d8-8d9d-946308b5a02c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19f63370-b823-58a5-b86b-cdc73de5787a" :question/math-academy-id "q-83721" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of $(x-3)^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ at $x=3?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(3)+{f}^{′}(3)(x-3)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(3)}{2}(x-3)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-3)^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{″}(3)}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(3) & =-\\frac{1}{(3)^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x-3)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(3)}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{9})=-\\frac{1}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184187 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "73571ff9-24a3-52b6-be57-3ef08652ebb7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First or Second Derivative of a Function at a Point Given Its Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c47d408-db9e-5327-9f7f-ce7eccb1fa16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348554} {:answer/id #uuid "5f07ef6d-85f2-59b3-a96f-7b8b9eac42cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349320} {:answer/id #uuid "6693e6cc-cfe6-5a95-8cae-72a9d0db8436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349385} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9ed999-0105-51ce-bc9f-bfc6c9efa24f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349717} {:answer/id #uuid "b02b92e0-aed3-5fc6-8493-f99eb2e9c996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f07ef6d-85f2-59b3-a96f-7b8b9eac42cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349320} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0cb7f31-3a71-5a13-b0f7-ae1f892a275f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "621b7ad2-ca4a-5dee-ad15-5637d15120d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-83725" :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is given by ${P}_{2}(x)=4+3(x+1)+(x+1)^{2}.$ What is the value of ${f}^{′}(-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "This is an expansion about the point $x=-1,$ so the coefficient of the first-degree term is ${f}^{′}(-1).$ \n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the first-degree term is $3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(-1)=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184188 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fce4cd5a-537a-5eb4-a610-59d2bad95a89" :knowledge-point/title "Deducing the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial Given the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07d09b0f-94f8-57f3-a3c4-2ed740107cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2-3(x-3)-2(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348512} {:answer/id #uuid "174e9f23-8532-5869-8b6e-0ce0c5e8b09e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+2(x-3)-(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348643} {:answer/id #uuid "b23e9637-0830-59f3-a497-9067d51e83bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-2(x-3)+3(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350023} {:answer/id #uuid "c153b651-e837-5860-a15c-11fc406258a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+3(x-3)+3(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350160} {:answer/id #uuid "f6eb14bf-75c0-5e93-a513-f5c5c5422f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+2(x-3)+(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "174e9f23-8532-5869-8b6e-0ce0c5e8b09e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+2(x-3)-(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348643} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd696db3-d2e4-51d5-983a-ae74102cc205" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88d8590c-5e74-5b68-b9b4-57dcc2ddc2e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-83728" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bb52a7975c917c7fe310eb29ae1daaa3438a906b84771ae5ce6998c055c4e6f7.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=3?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(3)+{f}^{′}(3)(x-3)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(3)}{2}(x-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(3)>0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(3)>0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(3)<0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=3.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=3+2(x-3)-(x-3)^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184188 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fce4cd5a-537a-5eb4-a610-59d2bad95a89" :knowledge-point/title "Deducing the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial Given the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01830617-2f47-57a1-adb5-c5db6a64a9e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4-2(x+2)-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348467} {:answer/id #uuid "1bf46842-043e-53b2-a8b9-a5dbc4afe66c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+(x+2)+2(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348687} {:answer/id #uuid "8194c134-c301-5ebb-87b9-5d95521ebbf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(x+2)-2(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349642} {:answer/id #uuid "bf3d9bc2-f9ff-59f3-9a20-8a18bd45e3ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+2(x+2)-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350144} {:answer/id #uuid "cc07ba6a-d144-5bb3-8b43-c8e93fa07921" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3+3(x+2)+(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf3d9bc2-f9ff-59f3-9a20-8a18bd45e3ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+2(x+2)-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350144} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2cd3b1f-2874-5020-af5c-0c4d2f680e11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0be5907a-6435-5c1a-99a6-1a8c86cd0ccc" :question/math-academy-id "q-83729" :question/problem "![](images/9f/9fe49372324dac217bb8e96ae6f485fe74308184ec9dd197fc98b973560dd8af.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=-2?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x+2)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-2)}{2}(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-2)>0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(-2)>0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-2$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-2)<0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=3+2(x+2)-(x+2)^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083306 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "918b64ff-b732-50dc-9158-806369dd83c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399210} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6e5666-ea13-5af7-b833-a4815ec380df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399211} {:answer/id #uuid "3009444f-bfd8-532d-8128-d1d01ae05164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399212} {:answer/id #uuid "3576f6c2-83e5-556b-a02f-499e53dc7e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399213} {:answer/id #uuid "36a5002c-c0b9-58ab-acb7-d95d0cb5a0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "918b64ff-b732-50dc-9158-806369dd83c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399210} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7146fa8-f4db-5ae1-a574-b50b6f3b88e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ee6c969-d319-57a0-b1a1-3435c9110a6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-83826" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=7{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x+6?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $6$ and the leading coefficient is $7.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }6}{\\text{factor of }7}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-7$ does not take the above form. The value $-7$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{7}{1},$ and the numerator $(7)$ is not a factor of $6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2785b5bd-88db-544d-a41d-b69d35be99ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348787} {:answer/id #uuid "354f131e-ae5c-51ab-8348-a87034b594f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348929} {:answer/id #uuid "4f24dbbe-b87e-5b97-894f-05bae0099c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349162} {:answer/id #uuid "75e8d951-cd2e-5911-8618-a190ffa28f20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349532} {:answer/id #uuid "faa13be6-1fba-5e10-bc32-18634ce1a08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2785b5bd-88db-544d-a41d-b69d35be99ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348787} :answer-field/id #uuid "74f262d7-769f-525a-a77b-2e82355e8aef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8324b04-6c39-5b50-ae14-3bae7af8be74" :question/math-academy-id "q-83840" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{5}+21{x}^{3}+16x+14,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $14,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 7,$ and $\\pm 14.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{7}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{2}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{14}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{14}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 7, & \\pm \\frac{7}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 14, & \\pm 7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm \\frac{7}{2}\\,\\pm 7,\\,\\pm 14.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157301 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "697f82e2-e564-522b-a46b-08313bf1aaf1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Expected Value of a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86917b21-fa6f-566b-a22a-c89ffe9b991b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{c}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155938} {:answer/id #uuid "c666cb7b-6db6-5b1e-84a7-43d138e42c76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155939} {:answer/id #uuid "e76dca8e-298c-543e-9305-5fe7d1b6bbda" :answer/type {:db/id 